#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111825 :tutorial/content "Suppose we wish to plot the graph of $y=2^x$. First, we create a table of values:\n\n| $x$ | $-3$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $y = 2^x$ | $\\dfrac18$ | $\\dfrac14$ | $\\dfrac12$ | $1$ | $2$ | $4$ | $8$ |\n\nNow, we can plot the graph:\n\n![](images/e9/e987a8647092c836bde4bcea31dc075e80e9da36a67133fc2b9431b99c5e42ed.png)\n\nNote the following key points:\n\n1. The graph has a $y$-intercept at $(0,1)$.\n2. The graph is increasing.\n3. For $x>0$, the graph grows very rapidly as $x$ increases. Symbolically, we write $y\\rightarrow \\infty$ as $x\\rightarrow \\infty$.\n4. For $x<0$, the graph gets very close to zero as $x$ decreases. However, it never actually reaches zero, so the graph has the horizontal asymptote $y=0$. Symbolically, we write $y\\rightarrow 0$ as $x\\rightarrow -\\infty$.\n5. The domain is $x\\in (-\\infty, \\infty)$, and the range is $y>0$.\n\nThese key points apply to *any* exponential curve $y=a^x$ with $a>1$. These graphs always have the same general shape:\n\n![](images/48/48f7bc7bb25bf1955d1e2cf48cffbf09e17f80a1228cada1c31f7ad4822bdace.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "1a4d886d-d7b8-5667-b52a-2c2eb4a35b91" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1540 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111826 :tutorial/content "Multiplying an exponential growth function by a positive number **scales** the graph in the vertical direction.\n\nFor example, the curve $y=2(3^x)$ represents a vertical scaling of $y=3^x$ by a factor of $2$. It takes all of the $y$-coordinates on $y=3^x$ and multiplies them by $2$. This has the effect of **stretching** the curve upwards.\n\n![](images/49/49c2136298e6fbf1adcc4c24ddef6d3daf47367d4d3f15dc18c476dff086087a.png)\n\nNotice that the horizontal asymptote at $y=0$ remained the same, and the $y$-intercept moved from $(0,1)$ to $(0,2)$.\n\nIn general, for *any* vertically stretched exponential curve $y=k(a^x)$,\n\n- the horizontal asymptote at $y=0$ remains the same, and\n- the $y$-intercept moves from $(0,1)$ to $(0,k)$." :tutorial/id #uuid "8c1fa510-e5f3-585b-852a-c46724144ec1" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1541 :tutorial/title "Vertical Stretching"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111833 :tutorial/content "The curve\n\n$$\ny=\\dfrac{1}{3}(3^x)\n$$\n\nrepresents a vertical scaling of $y=3^x$ by a factor of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nIt takes all of the $y$-coordinates on $y=3^x$ and multiplies them by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nThis has the effect of squashing, or **compressing**, the curve downwards.\n\n![](images/e3/e3ae2f177dd60050dd4c4035c66ebfc46d1028eed74f1d23178ddaae35d35378.png)\n\nNotice that the horizontal asymptote at $y=0$ remained the same, and the $y$-intercept moved from $(0,1)$ to\n\n$$\n\\left(0,\\dfrac{1}{3} \\right)\n$$\n\nIn general for *any* vertically stretched exponential curve $y=k(a^x)$,\n\n- the horizontal asymptote at $y=0$ remains the same, and\n- the $y$-intercept moves from $(0,1)$ to $(0,k)$." :tutorial/id #uuid "78c17e05-8ef3-58fd-a9ea-a2fc04f4bd83" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1858 :tutorial/title "Vertical Compression"}]}
